  "evidence": "TEXT_E: Right to see a GP within seven days (24 hours if urgent) with 8,000 more GPs; guaranteed urgent NHS dentistry; mental health hubs in schools; free personal care, a Carer’s Minimum Wage and paid carer’s leave; expand free school meals to children in poverty; strengthen disability rights and end disproportionate Stop and Search; Net Zero by 2045, a Clean Air Act, and binding targets to end sewage dumping; back entrepreneurs and small firms, apprenticeships and Lifelong Skills Grants; fair‑tax reforms (windfall tax on oil and gas, reversing big bank cuts, CGT reform, taxing share buybacks); humane asylum (scrap Illegal Migration Act/Rwanda, safe and legal routes, work after three months); community policing and NATO commitment. TEXT_B: Freeze non‑essential immigration, leave ECHR, swift detention/deportation, and new detention places; 40,000 more police, tougher sentencing, and a patriotic curriculum; scrap Net Zero and green levies, expand oil/gas and shale; benefits tightening and a two‑strike job‑offer rule; large personal/business tax cuts and deregulation; prioritise locals for social housing and scrap the Equalities Act’s DE&I regime; NHS waiting‑list pledge with private care vouchers and tax relief.",
  "reasoning": "Because Care and Equality are the electorate’s top priorities, TEXT_E’s focus on universal and faster healthcare access, social care and poverty support, civil rights, and environmental health speaks most directly to Care/Harm and Equality, while its fair‑tax and small‑business measures address Proportionality. TEXT_B prioritises Loyalty/Authority (sovereignty, policing, immigration control) and some Proportionality via stricter welfare and tax cuts, but its reduced emphasis on equality measures and scrapping environmental protections make it a weaker fit for the given profile.",
